\title{Contractible Types and Contractible Maps}
\taxon{Type Theory}
\date{2026-07-18}
\import{macros}
\p{
    This note follows Chapter 10 of [Introduction to Homotopy Type Theory](hott-2022).
    Contractibility packages existence and coherent uniqueness in a form stable under equivalence.
}
\p{
    Saying that a type has an element only expresses existence. Saying that it has exactly one
    element in a set-theoretic sense is also too rigid for homotopy type theory, because equality
    itself may contain structure. A contractible type instead chooses a center and continuously
    identifies every point with that center. It is therefore a singleton \em{up to homotopy}.
}
\p{
    The same idea applies fiberwise. A fiber records an input together with a path showing that it
    maps to the chosen output. Contractibility of that fiber says not only that a preimage exists,
    but that the complete preimage data are coherently unique. This is the homotopical replacement
    for the conjunction of surjectivity and injectivity.
}
\subtree{
    \title{Contractible types}
    \transclude{def-005D}
    \p{
        The unit type is contractible, and every contractible type is equivalent to the unit type.
        Contractibility is stronger than mere inhabitation because it also controls all identity
        information.
    }
    \p{
        For any #{a:A}, the singleton type #{\Sigma_{x:A}(a=x)} is contractible with center
        #{(a,\refl_a)}. Its contraction is exactly path induction: every pair #{(x,p:a=x)} is
        identified with #{(a,\refl_a)}. This observation is the prototype for the fundamental
        theorem of identity types.
    }
}
\subtree{
    \title{Singleton induction}
    \transclude{def-005J}
    \p{
        The principle resembles induction for the unit type, but its computation rule is a path
        rather than a judgmental equality. Contractibility supplies enough paths to transport a
        value at the center to every other point; conversely, singleton induction applied to the
        family #{x\mapsto(a=x)} constructs a contraction.
    }
    \transclude{thm-0017}
}
\subtree{
    \title{Fibers and contractible maps}
    \transclude{def-005E}
    \transclude{def-005F}
    \p{
        The forward implication needs no coherence improvement: the contraction already compares
        the chosen center of each fiber with every other point of that fiber. The following proof
        extracts the section and retraction explicitly.
    }
    \transclude{thm-001B}
}
\subtree{
    \title{Equivalences are contractible maps}
    \p{
        The converse requires more care. An inverse chooses a plausible center
        #{(g(y),G(y))} for each fiber, but the two inverse homotopies need not initially satisfy the
        compatibility required for equality of dependent pairs. Coherent invertibility records
        precisely this missing triangle identity.
    }
    \transclude{def-005K}
    \transclude{thm-0018}
    \transclude{thm-0019}
    \transclude{thm-001A}
}
\subtree{
    \title{Why this formulation matters}
    \p{
        Fiberwise contractibility makes uniqueness explicit and behaves well under pullback and
        composition. It also prepares the later proof that #{\mathsf{isEquiv}(f)} is a proposition:
        once a fiber is contractible, there is no meaningful choice left in its center or contraction.
    }
}
